## sn12.62
sn12.62:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn12.62:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, an unlearned ordinary person might become disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed from this body made up of the four principal states. | T: There he addressed the monks, “Monks, an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person might grow disenchanted with this body composed of the four great elements, might grow dispassionate toward it, might gain release from it.
sn12.62:1.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.62:1.4 | S: This body made up of the four principal states is seen to accumulate and disperse, to be taken up and laid to rest. | T: Because the growth & decline, the taking up & putting down of this body composed of the four great elements are apparent.
sn12.62:1.5 | S: That’s why an unlearned ordinary person might become disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed from it. | T: Thus the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person might grow disenchanted, might grow dispassionate, might gain release there.
sn12.62:1.6 | S: But an unlearned ordinary person is unable to become disillusioned, dispassionate, or freed from that which is called ‘mind’ and also ‘sentience’ and also ‘consciousness’. | T: “But as for what’s called ‘mind,’ ‘intellect,’ or ‘consciousness,’ the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person is unable to grow disenchanted with it, unable to grow dispassionate toward it, unable to gain release from it.
sn12.62:1.7 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.62:1.8 | S: Because for a long time they’ve been attached to it, thought of it as their own, and mistaken it: | T: For a long time this has been relished, appropriated, and grasped by the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person as, ‘This is me, this is my self, this is what I am.’
sn12.62:1.9 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self.’ | T: (none)
sn12.62:1.10 | S: That’s why an unlearned ordinary person is unable to become disillusioned, dispassionate, or freed from it. | T: Thus the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person is unable to grow disenchanted with it, unable to grow dispassionate toward it, unable to gain release from it.
sn12.62:2.1 | S: But an unlearned ordinary person would be better off taking this body made up of the four principal states to be their self, rather than the mind. | T: “It would be better for the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person to hold to the body composed of the four great elements, rather than the mind, as the self.
sn12.62:2.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.62:2.3 | S: This body made up of the four principal states is seen to last for a year, or for two, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, or a hundred years, or even longer. | T: Because this body composed of the four great elements is seen standing for a year, two years, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, a hundred years or more.
sn12.62:2.4 | S: But that which is called ‘mind’ and also ‘sentience’ and also ‘consciousness’ arises as one thing and ceases as another all day and all night. | T: But what’s called ‘mind,’ ‘intellect,’ or ‘consciousness’ by day and by night arises as one thing and ceases as another. Just as a monkey, swinging through a forest wilderness, grabs a branch. Letting go of that, it grabs another branch. Letting go of that, it grabs another one. Letting go of that, it grabs another one. In the same way, what’s called ‘mind,’ ‘intellect,’ or ‘consciousness’ by day and by night arises as one thing and ceases as another.
sn12.62:3.1 | S: In this case, a learned noble disciple carefully and rationally applies the mind to dependent origination itself: | T: “The instructed disciple of the noble ones, (however,) attends carefully & appropriately right there at the dependent co-arising:
sn12.62:3.2 | S: ‘So, when this exists, this comes to be; due to the arising of this, this arises. | T: ‘When this is, that is. From the arising of this comes the arising of that.
sn12.62:3.3 | S: When this doesn’t exist, this doesn’t come to be; due to the cessation of this, this ceases.’ | T: When this isn’t, that isn’t. From the cessation of this comes the cessation of that.’
sn12.62:3.4 | S: Pleasant feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: “In dependence on a sensory contact that is to be felt as pleasure, monks, there arises a feeling of pleasure. When sensing a feeling of pleasure, one discerns that ‘I am sensing a feeling of pleasure.’
sn12.62:3.5 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as pleasant, the corresponding pleasant feeling ceases and stops. | T: One discerns that ‘With the cessation of that very sensory contact that is to be felt as pleasure, the concomitant feeling—the feeling of pleasure that has arisen in dependence on the sensory contact that is to be felt as pleasure—ceases, is stilled.’
sn12.62:3.6 | S: Painful feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as painful. | T: In dependence on a sensory contact that is to be felt as pain.…
sn12.62:3.7 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as painful, the corresponding painful feeling ceases and stops. | T: (none)
sn12.62:3.8 | S: Neutral feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as neutral. | T: In dependence on a sensory contact that is to be felt as neither pleasure nor pain, there arises a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain. When sensing a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain, one discerns that ‘I am sensing a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain.’
sn12.62:3.9 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as neutral, the corresponding neutral feeling ceases and stops. | T: One discerns that ‘With the cessation of that very sensory contact that is to be felt as neither pleasure nor pain, the concomitant feeling—the feeling of neither pleasure nor pain that has arisen in dependence on the sensory contact that is to be felt as neither pleasure nor pain—ceases, is stilled.’
sn12.62:4.1 | S: When you rub two sticks together, heat is generated and fire is produced. But when you part the sticks and lay them aside, any corresponding heat ceases and stops. | T: “Just as when, from the friction & conjunction of two fire sticks, heat is born and fire appears, and from the separation & disjunction of those very same fire sticks, the concomitant heat ceases, is stilled;
sn12.62:4.2 | S: In the same way, pleasant feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: in the same way, in dependence on a sensory contact that is to be felt as pleasure, there arises a feeling of pleasure.…
sn12.62:4.3 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as pleasant, the corresponding pleasant feeling ceases and stops. | T: (none)
sn12.62:4.4 | S: Painful feeling … | T: In dependence on a sensory contact that is to be felt as pain.…
sn12.62:4.5 | S: Neutral feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as neutral. | T: In dependence on a sensory contact that is to be felt as neither pleasure nor pain, there arises a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain.…
sn12.62:4.6 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as neutral, the corresponding neutral feeling ceases and stops. | T: One discerns that ‘With the cessation of that very sensory contact that is to be felt as neither pleasure nor pain, the concomitant feeling… ceases, is stilled.’
sn12.62:5.1 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with contact, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: “Seeing thus, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness.
sn12.62:5.2 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn12.62:5.3 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn12.62:5.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.55
an4.55:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
an4.55:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Bhaggas at Crocodile’s Bellow, in the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood. | T: Once the Blessed One was staying among the Bhaggas in the Deer Park at Bhesakaḷā Forest, near Crocodile Haunt.
an4.55:1.3 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went to the home of the householder Nakula’s father, where he sat down on the seat spread out. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One, having adjusted his under robe and carrying his bowl and outer robe, went to the home of the householder, Nakula’s father. On arrival, he sat down on a seat made ready.
an4.55:1.4 | S: Then the householder Nakula’s father and the housewife Nakula’s mother went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. Nakula’s father said to the Buddha, | T: Then Nakula’s father & Nakula’s mother went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, Nakula’s father said to the Blessed One:
an4.55:2.1 | S: “Sir, ever since we were both young, and Nakula’s mother was given to me in marriage, I can’t recall betraying her even in thought, still less in deed. | T: “Lord, ever since Nakula’s mother as a young girl was brought to me (to be my wife) when I was just a young boy, I am not conscious of being unfaithful to her even in mind, much less in body.
an4.55:2.2 | S: We want to see each other in both this life and the next.” | T: We want to see one another not only in the present life but also in the life to come.”
an4.55:2.3 | S: Then Nakula’s mother said to the Buddha, | T: And Nakula’s mother said to the Blessed One:
an4.55:2.4 | S: “Sir, ever since we were both young, and I was given in marriage to Nakula’s father, I can’t recall betraying him even in thought, still less in deed. | T: “Lord, ever since I as a young girl was brought to Nakula’s father when he was just a young boy, I am not conscious of being unfaithful to him even in mind, much less in body.
an4.55:2.5 | S: We want to see each other in both this life and the next.” | T: We want to see one another not only in the present life but also in the life to come.”
an4.55:3.1 | S: “Householders, if wife and husband want to see each other in both this life and the next, they should be equals in faith, ethics, generosity, and wisdom. | T: (The Blessed One said:) “If both husband & wife want to see one another not only in the present life but also in the life to come, they should be in tune (with each other) in conviction, in tune in virtue, in tune in generosity, and in tune in discernment. Then they will see one another not only in the present life but also in the life to come.”
an4.55:4.1 | S: When both are faithful and bountiful, | T: Husband & wife, both of them having conviction, being responsive,
an4.55:4.2 | S: disciplined, living righteously, | T: being restrained, living by the Dhamma,
an4.55:4.3 | S: then wife and husband | T: (none)
an4.55:4.4 | S: say nice things to each other. | T: addressing each other with loving words:
an4.55:5.1 | S: Their needs are amply satisfied, | T: they benefit in manifold ways.
an4.55:5.2 | S: so they live at ease. | T: To them comes bliss.
an4.55:5.3 | S: Their enemies are downhearted, | T: Their enemies are dejected
an4.55:5.4 | S: when both are equal in ethics. | T: when both are in tune in virtue.
an4.55:6.1 | S: Having practiced the teaching here, | T: Having followed the Dhamma here in this world,
an4.55:6.2 | S: both equal in precepts and observances, | T: both in tune in habits & practices,
an4.55:6.3 | S: they delight in the heavenly realm, | T: they delight in the world of the devas,
an4.55:6.4 | S: enjoying all the pleasures they desire.” | T: enjoying the pleasures they desire.
an4.55:6.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.56
an5.56:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to his own mentor, and said, | T: Then a certain monk went to his preceptor and, on arrival, said to him,
an5.56:1.2 | S: “Now, sir, my body feels like it’s drugged. I’m disorientated, the teachings don’t spring to mind, and dullness and drowsiness fill my mind. I lead the spiritual life dissatisfied, and have doubts about the teachings.” | T: “Venerable sir, it’s as if my body is drugged. I’ve lost my bearings. Things aren’t clear to me. My mind keeps being overwhelmed with sloth & torpor. I lead the holy life dissatisfied. I have uncertainty about the teachings.”
an5.56:2.1 | S: Then that mendicant took his protégé to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: So that monk [the preceptor], taking his student along, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he sat to one side.
an5.56:2.2 | S: “Sir, this mendicant says this: | T: As he was sitting there he [told the Blessed One what his student had said.]
an5.56:2.3 | S: ‘Now, sir, my body feels like it’s drugged. I’m disorientated, the teachings don’t spring to mind, and dullness and drowsiness fill my mind. I lead the spiritual life dissatisfied, and have doubts about the teachings.’” | T: (none)
an5.56:3.1 | S: “That’s how it is, mendicant, when your sense doors are unguarded, you eat too much, you’re not dedicated to wakefulness, you’re unable to discern skillful qualities, and you don’t pursue the development of the qualities on the side of awakening in the evening and toward dawn. Your body feels like it’s drugged. You’re disorientated, the teachings don’t spring to mind, and dullness and drowsiness fill your mind. You lead the spiritual life dissatisfied, and have doubts about the teachings. | T: [The Blessed One said:] “That’s how it is, monk, for one who lives with his senses unguarded, knowing no moderation in food, not committed to wakefulness, with no insight into skillful qualities, and not committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night. It’s as if his body is drugged. He’s lost his bearings. Things aren’t clear to him. His mind keeps being overwhelmed with sloth & torpor. He leads the holy life dissatisfied. He has uncertainty about the teachings.
an5.56:3.2 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, monk, you should train yourself:
an5.56:3.3 | S: ‘I will guard my sense doors, eat in moderation, be dedicated to wakefulness, discern skillful qualities, and pursue the development of the qualities on the side of awakening in the evening and toward dawn.’ | T: ‘I will have my senses guarded, know moderation in food, commit to wakefulness, gain insight into skillful qualities, and dwell committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night.’
an5.56:3.4 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourself.”
an5.56:4.1 | S: When that mendicant had been given this advice by the Buddha, he rose from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: Then that monk [the student], having been admonished by the Blessed One’s admonishment, rose from his seat, bowed down to him, and—after circumambulating him—left.
an5.56:4.2 | S: Then that mendicant, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Then—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time entered & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
an5.56:4.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
an5.56:4.4 | S: And that mendicant became one of the perfected. | T: And so that monk became one of the arahants.
an5.56:5.1 | S: When that mendicant had attained perfection, he went up to his own mentor, and said, | T: Then, having attained arahantship, he went to his preceptor and, on arrival, said to him,
an5.56:5.2 | S: “Now, sir, my body doesn’t feel like it’s drugged. I’m not disorientated, the teachings spring to mind, and dullness and drowsiness don’t fill my mind. I lead the spiritual life satisfied, and have no doubts about the teachings.” | T: “Venerable sir, my body is no longer drugged and I’ve regained my bearings. Things are clear to me. My mind is no longer overwhelmed with sloth & torpor. I lead the holy life satisfied. I have no uncertainty about the teachings.”
an5.56:5.3 | S: Then that mendicant took his protégé to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: So that monk [the preceptor], taking his student along, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he sat to one side.
an5.56:5.4 | S: “Sir, this mendicant says this: | T: As he was sitting there he [told the Blessed One what his student had said.]
an5.56:5.5 | S: ‘Now, sir, my body doesn’t feel like it’s drugged. I’m not disorientated, the teachings spring to mind, and dullness and drowsiness don’t fill my mind. I lead the spiritual life satisfied, and have no doubts about the teachings.’” | T: (none)
an5.56:6.1 | S: “That’s how it is, mendicant, when your sense doors are guarded, you’re moderate in eating, you’re dedicated to wakefulness, you’re able to discern skillful qualities, and you pursue the development of the qualities on the side of awakening in the evening and toward dawn. Your body doesn’t feel like it’s drugged. You’re not disorientated, the teachings spring to mind, and dullness and drowsiness don’t fill your mind. You lead the spiritual life satisfied, and have no doubts about the teachings. | T: [The Blessed One said:] “That’s how it is, monk, for one who lives with his senses guarded, knowing moderation in food, committed to wakefulness, with insight into skillful qualities, and committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night. His body is no longer drugged and he’s regained his bearings. Things are clear to him. His mind is no longer overwhelmed with sloth & torpor. He leads the holy life satisfied. He has no uncertainty about the teachings.
an5.56:6.2 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, monks, you should train yourselves:
an5.56:6.3 | S: ‘We will guard our sense doors, eat in moderation, be dedicated to wakefulness, discern skillful qualities, and pursue the development of the qualities on the side of awakening in the evening and toward dawn.’ | T: ‘We will have our senses guarded, know moderation in food, commit to wakefulness, gain insight into skillful qualities, and dwell committed to the development of the wings to awakening in the early & late hours of the night.’
an5.56:6.4 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
an5.56:6.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti97
iti97:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti97:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, in this teaching and training a mendicant of good morals, good practice, and good wisdom is called consummate, accomplished, a supreme person. | T: “Monks, a monk who has admirable virtue, admirable qualities, & admirable discernment is called, in this Dhamma-&-Vinaya, one who is complete, fulfilled, a superlative person.
iti97:3.1 | S: And how does a mendicant have good morals? | T: “And how is a monk a person with admirable virtue?
iti97:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
iti97:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant has good morals. | T: In this way a monk is a person with admirable virtue.
iti97:3.4 | S: Such is one of good morality. | T: Thus he is of admirable virtue.
iti97:4.1 | S: And how does one have good practice? | T: “And how is a monk a person with admirable qualities?
iti97:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates pursuing the development of the seven qualities on the side of awakening. | T: There is the case where a monk lives devoted to developing the seven [sets of] qualities that are wings to awakening.
iti97:4.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant has good practice. | T: In this way a monk is a person with admirable qualities.
iti97:4.4 | S: Such is one of good morality and good practice. | T: Thus he is of admirable virtue & admirable qualities.
iti97:5.1 | S: And how does one have good wisdom? | T: “And how is a monk a person with admirable discernment?
iti97:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: There is the case where a monk–with the ending of effluents–remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing it for himself right in the here-&-now.
iti97:5.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant has good wisdom; | T: In this way a monk is a person with admirable discernment.
iti97:6.1 | S: Such is one of good morals, good practice, and good wisdom, who in this teaching and training is called consummate, accomplished, a supreme person.” | T: Thus he is of admirable virtue, admirable qualities, admirable discernment. In this Dhamma-&-Vinaya he is called one who is complete, fulfilled, a superlative person.”
iti97:6.2 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti97:6.3 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti97:7.1 | S: “Who does nothing wrong | T: Devoid of wrong-doing
iti97:7.2 | S: by body, speech or mind, | T: in thought, word, or deed,
iti97:7.3 | S: is said to be one of good morals, | T: he’s called a person of admirable virtue:
iti97:7.4 | S: a conscientious mendicant. | T: the monk conscientious.
iti97:8.1 | S: Who has well developed the seven | T: Well-developed in the qualities
iti97:8.2 | S: factors that lead to awakening | T: that go to the attainment of self-awakening,
iti97:8.3 | S: is said to be one good practice, | T: he’s called a person of admirable qualities:
iti97:8.4 | S: an unpretentious mendicant. | T: the monk unassuming.
iti97:9.1 | S: Who understands for themselves | T: Discerning right here for himself, in himself,
iti97:9.2 | S: the end of suffering in this life | T: the ending of stress
iti97:9.3 | S: is said to be one good wisdom, | T: he’s called a person of admirable discernment:
iti97:9.4 | S: an undefiled mendicant. | T: the monk with no effluent.
iti97:10.1 | S: One accomplished in these three things, | T: Consummate in these things,
iti97:10.2 | S: untroubled, with doubts cut off, | T: untroubled, with doubt cut away,
iti97:10.3 | S: unattached to anything in the world, | T: unattached in all the world,
iti97:10.4 | S: has given up everything, they say.” | T: he’s said to have abandoned the All.
iti97:11.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti97:11.2 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti110
iti110:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti110:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while walking. | T: “If, while he is walking, monks, there arises in a monk a thought of sensuality, a thought of ill-will, or a thought of harmfulness,
iti110:2.2 | S: They tolerate it and don’t give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. Such a mendicant is said to be ‘not keen or prudent, always lazy, and lacking energy’ when walking. | T: and he does not quickly abandon, dispel, demolish, or wipe that thought out of existence, then a monk walking with such a lack of ardency & compunction is called continually & continuously lethargic & low in his persistence.
iti110:2.3 | P: Carampi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto anātāpī anottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ kusīto hīnavīriyoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti110:3.1 | S: Suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while standing … | T: “If, while he is standing.…
iti110:3.2 | P: Tañce, bhikkhave, bhikkhu adhivāseti nappajahati na vinodeti na byantīkaroti na anabhāvaṁ gameti. | T: (none)
iti110:3.3 | P: Ṭhitopi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto anātāpī anottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ kusīto hīnavīriyoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti110:4.1 | S: sitting … | T: “If, while he is sitting.…
iti110:4.2 | P: Tañce, bhikkhave, bhikkhu adhivāseti nappajahati na vinodeti na byantīkaroti na anabhāvaṁ gameti. | T: (none)
iti110:4.3 | P: Nisinnopi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto anātāpī anottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ kusīto hīnavīriyoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti110:5.1 | S: or when lying down while awake. | T: “If, while he is lying down, there arises in a monk a thought of sensuality, a thought of ill-will, or a thought of harmfulness,
iti110:5.2 | S: They tolerate it and don’t give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. | T: and he does not quickly abandon, dispel, demolish, or wipe that thought out of existence,
iti110:5.3 | S: Such a mendicant is said to be ‘not keen or prudent, always lazy, and lacking energy’ when lying down while awake. | T: then a monk lying down with such a lack of ardency & compunction is called continually & continuously lethargic & low in his persistence.
iti110:6.1 | S: Suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while walking. | T: “But if, while he is walking, there arises in a monk a thought of sensuality, a thought of ill-will, or a thought of harmfulness,
iti110:6.2 | S: They don’t tolerate them, but give them up, dispel them, eliminate them, and obliterate them. | T: and he quickly abandons, dispels, demolishes, & wipes that thought out of existence,
iti110:6.3 | S: Such a mendicant is said to be ‘keen and prudent, always energetic and resolute’ when walking. | T: then a monk walking with such ardency & compunction is called continually & continuously resolute, one with persistence aroused.
iti110:7.1 | S: Suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while standing … | T: “If, while he is standing.…
iti110:7.2 | P: Tañce, bhikkhave, bhikkhu nādhivāseti pajahati vinodeti byantīkaroti anabhāvaṁ gameti. | T: (none)
iti110:7.3 | P: Ṭhitopi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto ātāpī ottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ āraddhavīriyo pahitattoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti110:8.1 | S: sitting … | T: “If, while he is sitting.…
iti110:8.2 | P: Tañce, bhikkhave, bhikkhu nādhivāseti pajahati vinodeti byantīkaroti anabhāvaṁ gameti. | T: (none)
iti110:8.3 | P: Nisinnopi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto ātāpī ottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ āraddhavīriyo pahitattoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti110:9.1 | S: or when lying down while awake. | T: “If, while he is lying down, there arises in a monk a thought of sensuality, a thought of ill-will, or a thought of harmfulness,
iti110:9.2 | S: They don’t tolerate it, but give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. | T: and he quickly abandons, dispels, demolishes, & wipes that thought out of existence,
iti110:9.3 | S: Such a mendicant is said to be ‘keen and prudent, always energetic and resolute’ when lying down while awake.” | T: then a monk lying down with such ardency & compunction is called continually & continuously resolute, one with persistence aroused.”
iti110:9.4 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti110:9.5 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti110:10.1 | S: “Whether walking or standing, | T: Whether walking, standing,
iti110:10.2 | S: sitting or lying down, | T: sitting, or lying down,
iti110:10.3 | S: one who thinks a bad thought | T: whoever thinks evil thoughts,
iti110:10.4 | S: to do with the domestic life | T: related to the household life,
iti110:11.1 | S: is practicing the wrong way, | T: is following no path at all,
iti110:11.2 | S: lost among things that delude; | T: smitten with delusory things.
iti110:11.3 | S: such a mendicant is incapable | T: He’s incapable, a monk like this,
iti110:11.4 | S: of experiencing the highest awakening. | T: of touching superlative self-awakening.
iti110:12.1 | S: But one who, whether standing or walking, | T: But whoever– walking, standing,
iti110:12.2 | S: sitting or lying down, | T: sitting, or lying down–
iti110:12.3 | S: has calmed their thoughts, | T: overcomes thought,
iti110:12.4 | S: loving peace of mind; | T: delighting in the stilling of thought:
iti110:12.5 | S: such a mendicant is capable | T: he’s capable, a monk like this,
iti110:12.6 | S: of experiencing the highest awakening.” | T: of touching superlative self-awakening.
