## ud4.3
ud4.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud4.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was wandering among the Kosalans with a large community of monks.
ud4.3:1.3 | S: And then the Buddha left the road, went to the root of a certain tree, and sat down on the seat spread out. | T: Then, coming down from the road, he went to a certain tree, and on arrival sat down on a seat laid out.
ud4.3:2.1 | S: Then a certain cowherd went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. | T: A certain cowherd then went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
ud4.3:2.2 | S: The Buddha educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired him with a Dhamma talk. | T: As he was sitting there, the Blessed One, instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged him with Dhamma-talk.
ud4.3:3.1 | S: Then the cowherd said to the Buddha, | T: The cowherd–instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged by the Blessed One’s talk on Dhamma–said to him,
ud4.3:3.2 | S: “Sir, would the Buddha, together with the mendicant Saṅgha, please accept tomorrow’s meal from me?” | T: “Lord, may the Blessed One, together with the community of monks, acquiesce to my offer of tomorrow’s meal.”
ud4.3:3.3 | S: The Buddha consented with silence. | T: The Blessed One acquiesced with silence.
ud4.3:3.4 | S: Then, knowing that the Buddha had consented, the cowherd rose from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: Then the cowherd, understanding the Blessed One’s acquiescence, got up from his seat, bowed down to the Blessed One and left, circling him to the right.
ud4.3:4.1 | S: And when the night had passed the cowherd had plenty of thick milk-rice prepared in his own home, with fresh ghee. Then he had the Buddha informed of the time, saying, | T: Then, after the night had passed, the cowherd–having prepared in his own home a great deal of thick milk-rice porridge & fresh ghee–announced the time of the meal to the Blessed One:
ud4.3:4.2 | S: “Sir, it’s time. The meal is ready.” | T: “It’s time, lord. The meal is ready.”
ud4.3:5.1 | S: The Buddha robed up, took his bowl and robe and, together with the Saṅgha of monks, went to the house of that cowherd, where he sat down on the prepared seat in the dining hall. | T: So the Blessed One early in the morning adjusted his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went together with the community of monks to the cowherd’s home. On arrival, he sat down on a seat laid out.
ud4.3:5.2 | S: Then the cowherd served and satisfied the mendicant Saṅgha headed by the Buddha with his own hands with a thick milk-rice and fresh ghee. | T: The cowherd, with his own hand, served & satisfied the community of monks headed by the Blessed One with thick milk-rice porridge & fresh ghee.
ud4.3:5.3 | S: When the Buddha had eaten and washed his hand and bowl, the cowherd took a low seat and sat to one side. | T: Then, when the Blessed One had eaten and had rinsed his bowl & hands, the cowherd, taking a lower seat, sat down to one side.
ud4.3:5.4 | S: The Buddha then educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired him with a teaching, after which he got up and left. | T: As he was sitting there, the Blessed One, instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged him with Dhamma-talk, then got up from his seat & left.
ud4.3:5.5 | S: But soon after the Buddha had left, the cowherd was killed by a certain man in the gap between village perimeters. | T: Now, not long after the Blessed One’s departure, the cowherd was killed by a certain man between the boundaries of two villages.
ud4.3:6.1 | S: Then several mendicants went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: A large number of monks then went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they told him,
ud4.3:6.2 | P: “yena, bhante, gopālakena ajja buddhappamukho bhikkhusaṅgho appodakapāyāsena navena ca sappinā sahatthā santappito sampavārito so kira, bhante, gopālako aññatarena purisena sīmantarikāya jīvitā voropito”ti. | T: “The cowherd who today served & satisfied the community of monks headed by the Blessed One with thick milk-rice porridge & fresh ghee, has been killed, it is said, by a certain man between the boundaries of two villages.”
ud4.3:7.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud4.3:8.1 | S: “A wrongly directed mind | T: Whatever an enemy might do to an enemy, or a foe to a foe, the ill-directed mind
ud4.3:8.2 | S: would do you more harm | T: can do to you even worse.
ud4.3:8.3 | S: than a hater to the hated, | T: (none)
ud4.3:8.4 | S: or an enemy to their foe.” | T: (none)
ud4.3:8.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.6
ud7.6:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.6:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.6:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Koṇḍañña Who Understood was sitting not far from the Buddha, cross-legged, with his body straight, reviewing the freedom through the ending of craving. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Aññāta Koṇḍañña was sitting not far from the Blessed One, his legs crossed, his body held erect, reflecting on [his] release through the total ending of craving.
ud7.6:2.1 | S: The Buddha saw him meditating there. | T: The Blessed One saw Ven. Aññāta Koṇḍañña sitting not far away, his legs crossed, his body held erect, reflecting on [his] release through the total ending of craving.
ud7.6:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.6:4.1 | S: “There is no root or ground or leaves for them, | T: For someone with no root, no soil, no leaves
ud7.6:4.2 | S: so where would creepers sprout from? | T: –how creepers?
ud7.6:4.3 | S: That attentive one is released from bonds: | T: Who’s fit to criticize him?– the enlightened one freed from bonds.
ud7.6:4.4 | S: who is worthy to criticize them? | T: (none)
ud7.6:4.5 | S: Even the gods praise them, | T: Even devas praise him.
ud7.6:4.6 | S: and by the Divinity, too, they’re praised.” | T: Even by Brahmā he’s praised.
ud7.6:4.7 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn11.15
sn11.15:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn11.15:1.2 | S: And then Sakka, lord of gods, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him, | T: Then Sakka the deva-king went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, stood to one side. As he was standing there, he said to the Blessed One,
sn11.15:1.3 | S: “Sir, what is a delightful place?” | T: “What, lord, is a delightful place?”
sn11.15:2.1 | S: “Shrines in parks and forests, | T: The Buddha: “Park shrines, forest shrines,
sn11.15:2.2 | S: well-made lotus ponds, | T: well-constructed lotus ponds
sn11.15:2.3 | S: are not worth a sixteenth part | T: aren’t worth one-sixteenth
sn11.15:2.4 | S: of a delightful human being. | T: of a delightful human being.
sn11.15:3.1 | S: Whether in village or wilderness, | T: In village or wilds,
sn11.15:3.2 | S: in a valley or the uplands, | T: valley, plateau:
sn11.15:3.3 | S: wherever the perfected ones live | T: that place is delightful where arahants dwell.”
sn11.15:3.4 | S: is a delightful place.” | T: (none)

## sn20.7
sn20.7:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī.
sn20.7:1.2 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, the Dasārahas had a <em>mridangam</em> drum called the Plugger. | T: “Monks, there once was a time when the Dasārahas had a large drum called ‘Summoner.’
sn20.7:1.3 | S: When the Plugger cracked, the Dasārahas patched it with another plug. | T: Whenever Summoner was split, the Dasārahas inserted another peg in it,
sn20.7:1.4 | S: Eventually, there came a time when the old wooden shell for the drumskin disappeared | T: until the time came when Summoner’s original wooden body had disappeared and only a conglomeration of pegs remained.
sn20.7:1.5 | S: and all that was left of the <em>mridangam</em> Plugger was a mass of plugs. | T: (none)
sn20.7:1.6 | S: In the same way, in a future time there will be mendicants who won’t want to listen when discourses spoken by the Realized One—deep, profound, transcendent, dealing with emptiness—are being recited. They won’t actively listen or try to understand, nor will they think those teachings are worth learning and memorizing. | T: [The Commentary notes that the drum originally could be heard for twelve leagues, but in its final condition couldn’t be heard even from behind a curtain.] “In the same way, in the course of the future there will be monks who won’t listen when discourses that are words of the Tathāgata—deep, deep in their meaning, transcendent, connected with emptiness—are being recited. They won’t lend ear, won’t set their hearts on knowing them, won’t regard these teachings as worth grasping or mastering.
sn20.7:2.1 | S: But when discourses composed by poets—poetry, with fancy words and phrases, composed by outsiders or spoken by disciples—are being recited they will want to listen. They’ll actively listen and try to understand, and they’ll think those teachings are worth learning and memorizing. | T: But they will listen when discourses that are literary works—the works of poets, elegant in sound, elegant in rhetoric, the work of outsiders, words of disciples—are recited. They will lend ear and set their hearts on knowing them. They will regard these teachings as worth grasping & mastering.
sn20.7:2.2 | S: And that is how the discourses spoken by the Realized One—deep, profound, transcendent, dealing with emptiness—will disappear. | T: “In this way the disappearance of the discourses that are words of the Tathāgata—deep, deep in their meaning, transcendent, connected with emptiness—will come about.
sn20.7:2.3 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Thus you should train yourselves:
sn20.7:2.4 | S: ‘When discourses spoken by the Realized One—deep, profound, transcendent, dealing with emptiness—are being recited we will want to listen. We will actively listen and try to understand, and we will think those teachings are worth learning and memorizing.’ | T: ‘We will listen when discourses that are words of the Tathāgata—deep, deep in their meaning, transcendent, connected with emptiness—are being recited. We will lend ear, will set our hearts on knowing them, will regard these teachings as worth grasping & mastering.’
sn20.7:2.5 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
sn20.7:2.6 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.23
sn35.23:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: (none)
sn35.23:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the all. | T: “Monks, I will teach you the All.
sn35.23:1.3 | S: Listen … | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn35.23:1.4 | S: And what is the all? | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One. The Blessed One said, “What is the All?
sn35.23:1.5 | S: It’s just the eye and sights, the ear and sounds, the nose and smells, the tongue and tastes, the body and touches, and the mind and ideas. | T: Simply the eye & forms, ear & sounds, nose & aromas, tongue & flavors, body & tactile sensations, intellect & ideas.
sn35.23:1.6 | S: This is called the all. | T: This, monks, is called the All.
sn35.23:1.7 | S: Mendicants, suppose someone was to say: | T: Anyone who would say,
sn35.23:1.8 | S: ‘I’ll deny this all and describe another all.’ They’d have no grounds for that claim, | T: ‘Repudiating this All, I will describe another,’
sn35.23:1.9 | S: they’d be stumped by questions, and, in addition, they’d get frustrated. | T: if questioned on what exactly might be the grounds for his statement, would be unable to explain and, furthermore, would be put to grief.
sn35.23:1.10 | S: Why is that? | T: Why?
sn35.23:1.11 | S: Because they’re out of their element.” | T: Because it lies beyond range.”
sn35.23:1.12 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn46.5
sn46.5:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: (none)
sn46.5:1.2 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then a certain monk approached the Blessed One and, on arrival, bowed down to him and sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
sn46.5:1.3 | S: “Sir, they speak of the ‘awakening factors’. | T: “Lord, ‘factors for awakening, factors for awakening,’ it is said.
sn46.5:1.4 | S: How are the awakening factors defined?” | T: To what extent are they said to be factors for awakening?”
sn46.5:1.5 | S: “Mendicant, they’re called awakening factors because they lead to awakening. | T: “They lead to awakening, monk. Therefore they are said to be factors for awakening.
sn46.5:1.6 | S: A mendicant develops the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: “There is the case where a monk develops mindfulness as a factor for awakening dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go. He develops analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening… persistence as a factor for awakening… rapture as a factor for awakening… calm as a factor for awakening… concentration as a factor for awakening…
sn46.5:1.7 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: equanimity as a factor for awakening dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go.
sn46.5:1.8 | S: As they develop the seven awakening factors, their mind is freed from the defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn, and ignorance. | T: “When these factors for awakening are developed, the mind is released from the effluent of sensuality, the mind is released from the effluent of becoming, the mind is released from the effluent of ignorance.
sn46.5:1.9 | S: When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn46.5:1.10 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: One discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.’
sn46.5:1.11 | S: They’re called awakening factors because they lead to awakening.” | T: “They lead to awakening, monk. Therefore they are said to be factors for awakening.”
sn46.5:1.12 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn48.46
sn48.46:1.1 | S: The same setting. | T: (none)
sn48.46:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, how many faculties must a mendicant develop and cultivate so that they can declare enlightenment: | T: “Through the development & pursuit of how many faculties, monks, does a monk whose effluents are ended declare gnosis:
sn48.46:1.3 | S: ‘I understand: “Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place”’?” | T: ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world’?”
sn48.46:2.1 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. …” | T: “For us, lord, the teachings have the Blessed One as their root, their guide, & their arbitrator. It would be good if the Blessed One himself would explicate the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from the Blessed One, the monks will remember it.”
sn48.46:2.2 | S: “A mendicant must develop and cultivate two faculties so that they can declare enlightenment. | T: “Monks, it’s through the development & pursuit of two faculties that a monk whose effluents are ended declares gnosis:
sn48.46:2.3 | P: ‘khīṇā jāti, vusitaṁ brahmacariyaṁ, kataṁ karaṇīyaṁ, nāparaṁ itthattāyā’ti pajānāmīti. | T: ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.’
sn48.46:2.4 | S: What two? | T: Through which two?
sn48.46:2.5 | S: Noble wisdom and noble freedom. | T: Through noble discernment & noble release.
sn48.46:2.6 | S: For their noble wisdom is the faculty of wisdom. | T: Whatever is his noble discernment is his faculty of discernment.
sn48.46:2.7 | S: And their noble freedom is the faculty of immersion. | T: Whatever is his noble release is his faculty of concentration.
sn48.46:2.8 | S: These are the two faculties that a mendicant must develop and cultivate so that they can declare enlightenment: | T: “It’s through the development & pursuit of these two faculties that a monk whose effluents are ended declares gnosis:
sn48.46:2.9 | S: ‘I understand: “Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.”’” | T: ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.’”
sn48.46:2.10 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn7.2
sn7.2:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn7.2:1.2 | S: The brahmin Bhāradvāja the Rude heard a rumor that | T: Then the brahman Akkosaka [“Insulter”] Bhāradvāja heard that a brahman of the Bhāradvāja clan had gone forth from the home life into homelessness in the presence of the Blessed One.
sn7.2:2.1 | S: a brahmin of the Bhāradvāja clan had gone forth from the lay life to homelessness in the presence of the ascetic Gotama. Angry and displeased he went to the Buddha and abused and insulted him with rude, harsh words. When he had spoken, the Buddha said to him: | T: Angered & displeased, he went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, insulted & cursed him with rude, harsh words. When this was said, the Blessed One said to him:
sn7.2:2.2 | S: “What do you think, brahmin? | T: “What do you think, brahman?
sn7.2:2.3 | S: Do friends and colleagues, relatives and kin, and guests still come to visit you?” | T: Do friends & colleagues, relatives & kinsmen come to you as guests?”
sn7.2:2.4 | S: “Sometimes they do, worthy Gotama.” | T: “Yes, Master Gotama, sometimes friends & colleagues, relatives & kinsmen come to me as guests.”
sn7.2:2.5 | S: “Do you then serve them with fresh and cooked foods and savories?” | T: “And what do you think? Do you serve them with staple & non-staple foods & delicacies?”
sn7.2:2.6 | S: “Sometimes I do.” | T: “Yes, sometimes I serve them with staple & non-staple foods & delicacies.”
sn7.2:2.7 | S: “But if they don’t accept it, brahmin, who does it belong to?” | T: “And if they don’t accept them, to whom do those foods belong?”
sn7.2:2.8 | S: “In that case it still belongs to me.” | T: “If they don’t accept them, Master Gotama, those foods are all mine.”
sn7.2:2.9 | S: “In the same way, brahmin, when you abuse, harass, and attack us who do not abuse, harass, and attack, we don’t accept it. | T: “In the same way, brahman, that with which you have insulted me, who is not insulting; that with which you have taunted me, who is not taunting; that with which you have berated me, who is not berating: that I don’t accept from you.
sn7.2:2.10 | S: It still belongs to you, brahmin, | T: It’s all yours, brahman.
sn7.2:2.11 | S: it still belongs to you! | T: It’s all yours.
sn7.2:3.1 | S: Someone who, when abused, harassed, and attacked, abuses, harasses, and attacks in return is said to eat the food and have a reaction to it. | T: “Whoever returns insult to one who is insulting, returns taunts to one who is taunting, returns a berating to one who is berating, is said to be eating together, sharing company, with that person.
sn7.2:3.2 | S: But we neither eat your food nor do we have a reaction to it. | T: But I am neither eating together nor sharing your company, brahman.
sn7.2:3.3 | S: It still belongs to you, brahmin, | T: It’s all yours.
sn7.2:3.4 | S: it still belongs to you!” | T: It’s all yours.”
sn7.2:3.5 | S: “The king and his retinue believe of the worthy Gotama, | T: “The king together with his court know this of Master Gotama—
sn7.2:3.6 | S: ‘the ascetic Gotama is a perfected one’. | T: ‘Gotama the contemplative is an arahant’—
sn7.2:3.7 | S: And yet he still gets angry.” | T: and yet still Master Gotama gets angry.”
sn7.2:4.1 | S: “Whence anger for one free of anger, | T: The Buddha: “Whence is there anger for one free from anger,
sn7.2:4.2 | S: tamed, living justly, | T: tamed, living in tune—
sn7.2:4.3 | S: freed by right knowledge, | T: one released through right knowing,
sn7.2:4.4 | S: peaceful and unaffected? | T: calmed & Such.
sn7.2:5.1 | S: When you get angry at an angry person | T: You make things worse when you flare up at someone who’s angry.
sn7.2:5.2 | S: you just make things worse for yourself. | T: (none)
sn7.2:5.3 | S: When you don’t get angry at an angry person | T: Whoever doesn’t flare up at someone who’s angry
sn7.2:5.4 | S: you win a battle hard to win. | T: wins a battle hard to win.
sn7.2:6.1 | S: When you know that the other is angry, | T: You live for the good of both —your own, the other’s— when, knowing the other’s provoked,
sn7.2:6.2 | S: you act for the good of both | T: (none)
sn7.2:6.3 | S: yourself and the other | T: (none)
sn7.2:6.4 | S: if you’re mindful and stay calm. | T: you mindfully grow calm.
sn7.2:7.1 | S: People inexpert in the teaching | T: (none)
sn7.2:7.2 | S: consider one who heals both | T: When you work the cure of both
sn7.2:7.3 | S: oneself and the other | T: —your own, the other’s—
sn7.2:7.4 | S: to be a fool.” | T: those who think you a fool know nothing of Dhamma.”
sn7.2:8.1 | S: When he had spoken, Bhāradvāja the Rude said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, the brahman Akkosaka Bhāradvāja said to the Blessed One,
sn7.2:8.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! … | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
sn7.2:8.3 | S: I go for refuge to the worthy Gotama, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
sn7.2:8.4 | S: Sir, may I receive the going forth, the ordination in the worthy Gotama’s presence?” | T: Let me obtain the Going-forth in Master Gotama’s presence, let me obtain Acceptance (into the Saṅgha of monks).”
sn7.2:9.1 | S: And the brahmin Bhāradvāja the Rude received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: Then the brahman Akkosaka Bhāradvāja received the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence, he gained the Acceptance.
sn7.2:9.2 | S: Not long after his ordination, Venerable Bhāradvāja the Rude, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: And not long after his Acceptance—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time entered & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
sn7.2:9.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
sn7.2:9.4 | S: And Venerable Bhāradvāja became one of the perfected. | T: And so Ven. Bhāradvāja became another one of the arahants.


